00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:39.059
Learning about ecology can make the world
look like one big buffet, except all the dishes are each other.

00:00:39.059 --> 00:00:44.969
At the start, you have the photosynthetic
organisms churning sunlight into sugar, and

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:51.659
everything just cascades into culinary chaos
from there.

00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:58.969
There’s ciliates eating algae, ciliates
eating other ciliates, rotifers and fish and

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:01.269
everything else that comes after.

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:14.530
And somewhere, reigning comfortable from its
spot in the middle of it all is this, the copepod.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.550
At first glance, the copepod probably doesn’t
seem any more significant than any other animal.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.750
I mean, it looks a little strange, but crustaceans
usually do.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:30.140
There are more than 20,000 species of copepods
in the world, and these various species have

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:36.180
managed to find their way into just about
every type of water, no matter the temperature or the salinity.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.730
There are species in freshwater ponds, species
in oceans, and species deep in hydrothermal vents.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:48.820
And just as they’ve managed to find so many
different /places/ to live, so have they found

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.780
many different /ways/ to live.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.090
Some swim freely through those waters, others
pursue a more parasitic strategy.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:03.000
And some become hosts for parasites themselves,
like the Guinea worm, which enters the copepod

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.200
as a larva, and leaves ready to infect whoever
has eaten the copepod.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.340
But however many different types of copepods
are out there in the world, they do have a

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.790
few things in common.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:20.560
They hatch from eggs, beginning their lives
as a round, not fully formed version of itself.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.170
The copepod molts several times from this
larval stage, its body lengthening and forming

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:32.180
more pronounced divisions as it goes through
a youthful “copepodite” stage.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:37.410
And eventually, the copepod reaches its adult
form, its segmented body forming a tapered

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.580
shape encased in an armored exoskeleton.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.940
Copepods have a very simple eye that senses
light, which they use to orient themselves in water.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.680
A few species are even capable of something
like vision.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:57.280
But perhaps the copepod’s most essential
sensing comes from its two pairs of antennae—the

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.390
most noticeable of which is that almost comically
large set that you see here.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:07.710
And along the length of those antennae are
small, sensory hairs called setae that help

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.040
the copepod detect changes in its surroundings.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.370
Now, those antennae don’t just sense, they
also help the copepod mate.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:21.450
When a male copepod finds a female copepod
who he likes very, very much, he can bend

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.300
the antennae to grab her.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:28.900
And that might sound like a funny detail about
an equally funny-looking part of a copepod’s

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.130
body, but it’s also part of its success.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:37.250
When you’re a tiny animal trying to reproduce
in a big body of water, the vastness of your

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.590
surroundings makes it enormously difficult
to find a mate.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:46.010
It would be like trying to find a date on
Tinder, except like 99% of the profiles you’re

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.730
swiping through are just pictures of water.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.790
So when you find someone, you really have
to hold on to them.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.650
Copepods of course aren’t the only animals
facing this challenge.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:01.379
There are lots of animals in the ocean that
are just too tiny to overcome the ocean’s

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:08.450
currents and the way that water feels like
honey when you’re that size.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:13.480
As a collective, they’re called zooplankton,
and they spend their life drifting where the

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:18.019
currents take them in the hopes that food
will also be there.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:23.460
The zooplankton’s diet makes them a valuable
part of the ecology buffet because they’re

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.220
at that spot right next to the organisms who
start the whole thing out, munching away at

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.970
the diatoms and other photosynthesizers of
the water.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:42.010
In the case of copepods, scientists observed
individuals of one species eating around 11,000-373,000

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.440
diatoms every day!

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.840
And as beautiful as diatoms are, and as much
as we like to look at them, the world would

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.180
be very different if there was no one trying
to eat hundreds of thousands of them a day.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:05:00.310
It would be a buffet with diatoms spilling
out of the trays, leaving no room for anything else.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:06.780
But copepods and their fellow zooplankton

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:14.020
fill that essential ecological role, acting
like a middle man that connects diatoms to the rest of us.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:18.430
And somehow, over the millions of years that
copepods have been around, they have become

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:24.490
the preeminent zooplankton--a kind of king
amongst middlemen, dominating zooplankton

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:30.340
populations in many bodies of water all over
the world, including Antarctic lakes.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:35.250
In Lake Baikal in Russia, the deepest freshwater
lake in the world, copepods are estimated

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.050
to make up 96% of the zooplankton population.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.949
And out in the oceans, copepods reign supreme.

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:45.490
So, the question is how?

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:51.610
And the answer is probably much more complex
than any one single trait, especially if we

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:56.540
attempt side-by-side comparisons with every
single animal that makes up the zooplankton

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.729
of every single body of water.

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:05.009
But if we consider the ocean—the vast, vast
ocean, where food and mates both are difficult

00:06:05.009 --> 00:06:10.830
to come by—the copepod has a number of talents
that do help it survive.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:15.290
It is, for one, very sensitive to its surroundings.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:20.300
Those hairs in its antenna detect predators
and prey alike through changes in the water

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.480
velocity around it.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.210
If the copepod detects a predator, it can
quickly jump away, using 4 or 5 pairs of legs

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:35.710
to jump up to 1000 body lengths per second,
making the copepod much faster than other

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.520
animals of its size.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.020
And if the copepod detects prey, it can launch
itself into a quick ambush instead.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.050
So speed is one advantage that may help the
copepod survive.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:52.610
But this organism doesn’t rely on ambushing
alone to sustain itself.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:07:00.009
It’s also capable of a much more sustained
search for food by vibrating some of its appendages,

00:07:00.009 --> 00:07:07.570
which creates a feeding current that the copepod
can scan and dine upon like its own personal banquet.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:12.789
And these are seemingly small talents that
stack up, producing in its totality an animal

00:07:12.789 --> 00:07:21.160
that is capable of feeding, escaping, and
mating its way to success in the vast open ocean.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:27.169
And with each diatom the copepod eats, their
strange regime becomes only more influential

00:07:27.169 --> 00:07:34.070
because one day a fish will probably eat that
copepod, and eventually another animal, maybe

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:39.880
even you, will eat that fish--connecting us
all to a strange little crustacean in the

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:48.830
sea whose kingdom has no boundary.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:54.900
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

